WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.540
One factor can keep a neuron alive.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.400
How can the same factor kill another cell?

3
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:11.103
The last lesson left a rule.

4
00:00:11.923 --> 00:00:15.023
A target makes a limited amount of a survival factor.

5
00:00:16.003 --> 00:00:17.603
Neurons that fail to get enough die.

6
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:23.587
Now picture one sympathetic neuron, in a cluster of nerve cell bodies in the neck.

7
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:28.547
Its axon runs out to a target, such as a blood vessel or a gland.

8
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:33.557
The target makes only a small amount of nerve growth factor, NGF.

9
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.767
NGF is one of four mammalian neurotrophins.

10
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.700
NGF. Brain-derived neurotrophic factor, or BDNF.

11
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:45.760
Neurotrophin three.

12
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.600
And neurotrophin four.

13
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:51.693
Each is a dimer, two identical chains held together.

14
00:00:52.613 --> 00:00:53.753
All four share one fold.

15
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:57.433
A neuron reads them with two kinds of receptor.

16
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:00.387
First, three Trk receptors.

17
00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:03.007
TrkA, TrkB and TrkC.

18
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:08.650
Each is a receptor tyrosine kinase, and each prefers particular factors.

19
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.170
Chapter two matches them up.

20
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.750
Second, one shared receptor, p75.

21
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.490
It binds all four factors, and it has no kinase.

22
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.890
p75 is a relative of Fas, the death receptor you have met.

23
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.033
Like Fas, it carries a death domain.

24
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:32.353
That is a stretch inside the cell that can bind proteins leading toward apoptosis.

25
00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:36.023
But it does not use the same proteins Fas uses.

26
00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:39.987
What p75 does depends on its partners.

27
00:01:40.887 --> 00:01:43.147
With a partner called sortilin, it can kill.

28
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:48.227
NGF is made first as a longer precursor, proNGF.

29
00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.367
Its extra front stretch is the pro-domain.

30
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.660
Enzymes cut the pro-domain off to leave mature NGF.

31
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.100
Sortilin grips the pro-domain.

32
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.140
p75 with sortilin binds the uncut precursor tightly.

33
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.420
Two puzzles follow.

34
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.840
First, how does a neuron know which factor is its own?

35
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.720
Second, add mature NGF to cultured sympathetic neurons, and they survive.

36
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:19.473
Add the same protein before its pro-domain has been cut off.

37
00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:24.013
In Barbara Hempstead's 2001 experiments, the neurons died.

38
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:27.617
Same protein family, opposite fates.

39
00:02:28.257 --> 00:02:29.577
Where would you look for the difference?

40
00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:37.837
Look at the receptors that read it.

41
00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:41.417
Here is the one idea to carry through this lesson.

42
00:02:42.237 --> 00:02:43.697
The target supplies the factor.

43
00:02:44.557 --> 00:02:46.657
The neuron's receptors decide what it means.

44
00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.870
A Trk receptor reads a neurotrophin as stay alive.

45
00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:57.330
p75 can read it as die, and with sortilin it reads the uncut precursor that way.

46
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:01.613
Cutting the precursor removes the stretch sortilin grips.

47
00:03:02.533 --> 00:03:05.733
So the cut changes which receptors the factor works through best.

48
00:03:06.733 --> 00:03:12.013
How much uncut precursor reaches neurons in a normal embryo is still unsettled.

49
00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:17.487
So the takeaway: the factor comes from the target, but its meaning comes from the

50
00:03:17.487 --> 00:03:17.787
receptors.
